**3. Prior art: what the checked theorems actually establish**

**[proved] Aggarwal–Maurer, EUROCRYPT 2009, Theorem 1:** the factoring assumption implies the generic RSA assumption for the paper’s RSA-key distributions. This is a reduction, not an unconditional factoring lower bound. Their **Lemma 6** converts an \(L\)-step RSA-root SLP satisfying its success hypothesis into factoring with probability at least
\[
\frac{\mu}{8(L+\log e)}
\]
and arithmetic cost \(O(L^3+\log^3 e)\). Lemmas 7–8 address branching and randomness. The random-monic-quotient construction is directly relevant to Tracks A/B. [Conference paper](https://iacr.org/archive/eurocrypt2009/54790037/54790037.pdf).

**[proved] Jager–Schwenk, January 25, 2012 full version, Theorems 2 and 3:** a generic Jacobi-membership algorithm with advantage \(\delta\), using \(m\) ring operations, yields factoring probability at least
\[
\frac{\delta}{2(m^2+4m+3)}.
\]
The reduction runs it once, adds at most \(2m\) ring operations and \((m+2)^2\) GCDs, and samples two uniform units. Theorem 3 gives the corresponding quadratic-residuosity reduction for distinct odd-prime semiprimes. These are the full-version numbers, not interchangeable conference numbering. Their central warning is especially relevant: Jacobi symbols are easy using canonical integer representatives. [Full version](https://eprint.iacr.org/2009/621.pdf), [journal record](https://doi.org/10.1007/s00145-012-9120-y).

**[proved] Damgård–Koprowski, EUROCRYPT 2002, Theorem 1:** this is an unconditional **generic-group** strong-root bound. For an order distribution \(D\) on \((B,B+C]\), let \(\alpha(D)\) be the maximum point probability of the largest prime divisor, and \(\beta(D,M)\) its probability of being at most \(M\). With \(m\) queries, \(2m\le M\), and \(\log e\le v\), success is at most
\[
\begin{aligned}
&(m^3+m^2+mv+2v+m)\alpha(D)\\
&+(m^2+m+2)\beta(D,M)
+\frac{m^2+m+3}{M}+\frac{m^2}{B}.
\end{aligned}
\]
**Corollary 1** gives negligible success for their hard distribution families. Ring addition is unavailable in this model. [Primary paper](https://www.iacr.org/archive/eurocrypt2002/23320252/DKea2002.pdf).

**[proved] Boneh–Venkatesan, EUROCRYPT 1998, Theorems 3.1 and 3.4:** their straight-line and algebraic factoring reductions to low-exponent RSA can be converted into ordinary factoring algorithms of polynomially related running time when the number of radical steps is \(O(\log T)\), where \(T\) is the reduction’s time. This limits certain reductions; it does not prove factoring hard. [Author-hosted paper](https://crypto.stanford.edu/~dabo/pubs/papers/no_rsa_red.pdf).

**[proved] Maurer–Wolf, EUROCRYPT 1998, Theorem 10:** for cyclic-group order \(p^ts\), \(\gcd(p,s)=1\), extracting \(p^r\)-th roots with \(t>r\ge1\) remains generically hard even with a DH oracle. Writing \(d=\lceil t/r\rceil-1\), their proof bounds \(T\)-step success by
\[
\frac{((T+2)(T+1)+2)d}{2p}.
\]
Again, this is a group result, not a ring-plus-GCD factoring bound. [Primary paper](https://crypto.ethz.ch/publications/files/MauWol98e.pdf).

**[UNVERIFIED] Lipton’s exact numbered theorem and complete quantifiers remain unverified.** The accessible 1994 primary preview states that sufficiently short SLPs for polynomials with many distinct rational roots would undermine cryptographic factoring hardness. It starts from constant \(1\), introduces nonuniform Boolean circuits, and explicitly treats arbitrary integer constants as a conjectured extension. It should not be cited as an unrestricted uniform polynomial-time factoring theorem. [Primary preview](https://page-one.springer.com/pdf/preview/10.1007/3-540-58691-1_45).

**[proved] Cheng, Theorem 1, is conditional:** under the paper’s short-interval smoothness conjecture, a nonzero multiple of \(m!\) has a subexponential-length SLP which can be constructed probabilistically in subexponential time. It is neither an unconditional polynomial-time factorizer nor a theorem furnishing many integer roots. [Author-hosted paper](https://qcheng2023.github.io/paper/factorial.pdf).

**[UNVERIFIED] Brown’s printed concrete lower bound needs caution.** The revised *Lower Bounds for Straight Line Factoring*, §5.1 equation (5), concerns fixed modulus-independent integer SLPs. Its concrete §5.2 formula has probability/exposition issues, and the conclusion acknowledges reviewer-identified faults. It is a weak lower-bound lead, not a superpolynomial generic factoring theorem. [Primary record](https://eprint.iacr.org/2010/047).

**[proved] A clean restricted lower bound can nevertheless be established independently.** Let a fixed constant-only division-free SLP output \(I\), and let \(p,q\) be sampled from \(M\) primes in \((2^s,2^{s+1})\). If its GCD splits with probability \(\mu\), at least \(\mu M/2\) candidate primes divide \(I\), by the union bound. Thus
\[
\log_2|I|\ge \frac{\mu Ms}{2}.
\]
An \(L\)-operation constant-only SLP satisfies
\[
\log_2|I|\le2^{L-1},
\]
giving
\[
\boxed{L\ge1+\log_2(\mu Ms/2)=s+O_\mu(1).}
\]
This is linear in factor bit length and excludes modulus-dependent programs.

**[proved] There is a precise, restricted permanent barrier.** Suppose one proved superpolynomial lower bounds against **all nonuniform, modulus-independent, constant-only SLP splitters** with constant success on balanced prime-size distributions. Short factorial SLPs would contradict that bound: choose a factorial cutoff near the median candidate prime. Its GCD splits whenever exactly one factor lies below the cutoff, with probability approaching \(1/2\).

Such a lower bound would therefore imply factorial \(\tau\)-hardness. Bürgisser’s **Main Theorem 1.2** then implies superpolynomial **constant-free, division-free permanent** complexity. This is **Theorem 1.1 in the 2006 ECCC version**. The paper expressly distinguishes that conclusion from ordinary \(VP\ne VNP\). [ECCC primary version](https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2006/113/download), [journal version](https://doi.org/10.1007/s00037-009-0260-x).

**[proved] That barrier does not automatically apply to a uniform-only lower bound:** short factorial circuits need not have an efficient constructor. Nor does it imply the full \(\tau\)-conjecture.

**[UNVERIFIED] No published theorem establishing the requested general “uniform GR+gcd factoring iff marginal-skew SLPs” characterization was located.** None of the checked RSA, Jacobi, or group-root theorems establishes a superpolynomial factoring lower bound for the model in the question.

