   116	## 2. Mathematics
   117	
   118	### 2.1 The unified gap theorem (formulation: GPT-6.1-sol r3 §2; components proved in `trackA_analysis.md` §3 and `trackC_analysis.md` §3 O1)
   119	
   120	**Model.** `R` a commutative unital ring; `V` an `R`-module generated by `D` elements; `T ∈ End_R(V)`; `v ∈ V`;
   121	`λ : V → W` an `R`-linear map into an `R`-module. Put `u_t = λ(T^t v)` and `L(f) = λ(f(T)v)`. An *observable* is exactly
   122	this linear `L`. The state may be computed by any means, including repeated squaring, towers, CRT products and nonlinear
   123	intermediate arithmetic. But `V, T, v, λ` stay fixed as the index `t` (or the coefficients of `f`) vary.
   124	
   125	**Theorem (proved; determinant trick plus forward propagation plus Fitting decomposition).**
   126	1. `u_t` satisfies a monic constant-coefficient recurrence of order `≤ D`.
   127	2. `D` consecutive zeros force all later values to vanish.
   128	3. The same holds along arithmetic progressions, with `T` replaced by `T^b` (nonnegative exponents, or `T` invertible).
   129	4. Over a field, if the nilpotent part has dimension `e`, such a block forces `u_t = 0` for all `t ≥ e`. The proof gap
   130	   (zero block starting before `e`) was found by the r3 critic and closed (`trackA_analysis.md` §3.2).
   131	5. For scalar outputs over a field, the sharp parameter is the minimal realization dimension (Hankel rank).
   132	
   133	A polynomial readout of degree `d` linearizes onto `C(D+d, d)` coordinates. The theorem then bounds that lifted dimension,
   134	**not** `D` (corrected O1 corollary; counterexample in §2.4).
   135	
   136	**Instances in this project.** Track A Theorem A1 in its corrected form: an observable that isolates class `r mod M` on
   137	`[0, N]` and is nonzero on `X^i` needs `D ≥ min(M, i+1)`. Balanced Corollary 3′: `D ≥ min(M, p+1)`. Track C O1:
   138	fixed-dimension powers carrying the prefix predicate need order `≥ p + 1`, and for the interval predicate `≥ L + 1` (tight).
   139	Also the Hadamard-mask corollary E1, fixed-quotient projected coefficient sequences, and fixed-matrix Krylov/Hankel sequences.
   140	
   141	**Exact boundary against known algorithms** (r3 §2 table; checked against the cited algorithm descriptions by GPT-6.1-sol):
   142	
   143	| Algorithm | Relation to the model |
   144	|---|---|
   145	| Pollard `p−1` | Its scalar power is *inside* (`a^t − 1` has a dimension-2 realization). But its zero set is periodic (an order condition), not an exact prefix or class mask, so the gap bound does not apply. Boundary check: `gcd(2^6 − 1, 77) = 7` with one scalar power. A "unified" theorem demanding `D ≥ 7` here would have smuggled in a false faithful-mask assumption. |
   146	| ECM | Curve arithmetic is nonlinear in the coordinates. Point annihilation plus gcd extraction does not supply a faithful linear zero pattern. Outside. |
   147	| BGS / Pollard–Strassen | The factorial recurrence `u_{K+1} = (K+1)u_K` has an index-dependent transition. Product trees, shifts and multipoint evaluation are outside the fixed-transition hypothesis. |
   148	| Harvey / Harvey–Hittmeir | Collision searches over growing collections of powers plus integer arithmetic. Individual power sequences can be inside; the decoder is outside. |
   149	| QS / NFS | Smoothness testing, integer or number-field relations and parity dependencies are not one fixed linear-power observable with the required zero pattern. Outside. |
   150	| Coppersmith | Integer lattices and size bounds recover small roots. Modular evaluation subroutines do not capture that extraction. Outside. |
   151	
   152	The theorem says nothing about gcds, pivots, adaptive decoders or nonlinear predicates. That is why it is an obstruction for
   153	specific access models, not a factoring lower bound. The general SLP version of the question is equivalent to open problems
   154	(Shub–Smale Theorem 2, Lipton, Bürgisser Thm 1.1/1.2), so an unconditional obstruction there is out of reach
   155	(`trackC_analysis.md` §3).
