   417	## 5. Continuation: the single most decisive next question
   418	
   419	> **In the ring-plus-gcd model (opaque residues mod N, ring operations, equality and gcd tests, polynomially many steps),
   420	> which contains every mechanism this project built, is deciding `ε = (−1/p)` for semiprimes `N ≡ 1 (mod 4)` (equivalently:
   421	> bit 3 of `φ(N)`, or "N is a sum of two squares") as hard as factoring?**
   422	
   423	**Why this question.** The project's negative results all live inside ring-plus-gcd access: sections, sketches, selectors,
   424	BM, folds, lifts. On the decoder side, every format needs about `n/4` bits beyond the information floor, and the cheapest
   425	format's first such bit is `ε`. Either outcome redirects the program:
   426	* A proof of generic factoring-equivalence (in the style of Jager–Schwenk's QR result, but for the fixed element `−1`)
   427	  closes the whole `R_N`-algebra program at the interface. Any future mechanism must then use non-generic access to integer
   428	  representatives (as Jacobi symbols, continued fractions and lattices do), and the project's tracks should be retired.
   429	* A generic algorithm with advantage `1/poly(n)` that does not factor would be the first source of a non-floor bit, and it
   430	  would immediately be tested on the next tower bit (`QR_2`).
   431	
   432	**Concrete attack.**
   433	1. *Theory (main line).* Adapt Jager–Schwenk (eprint 2009/621 full version, Theorems 2–3) to the fixed element `−1`. Their
   434	   reduction re-randomizes the input element; here the input is constant, so the program is a constant-only SLP with
   435	   equality tests. Combine the transcript-freezing argument (r2 §1 notes it can lose probability for adaptive programs) with
   436	   the constant-only SLP bound (r2 §3) and the CRT-disagreement equivalence (r2 §1). Target lemma: any `T`-step generic
   437	   algorithm deciding `ε` with advantage `δ` yields a factor with probability `≥ δ/poly(T)`. Have GPT-6.1-sol attempt the
   438	   proof blind, and an independent Opus critic audit it.
   439	2. *Cheap falsification experiment (parallel).* A pre-registered correlation battery. Freeze a menu of statistics from the
   440	   existing code: Track A section values, Track B sketch coefficients, ranks and residues, BM lengths, and also non-generic
   441	   statistics (continued-fraction period features of `√N`, reduced-form counts of `Cl(−4N)` restricted to small `a`). Run on
   442	   `10^5` balanced `N ≡ 1 (mod 4)` at 32, 48 and 64 bits. Refutation rule: no statistic exceeds a Bonferroni-corrected
   443	   `5σ` correlation with `ε`. Cost: a few CPU-hours. Expected outcome: no signal. A signal would be checked for leakage first.
   444	
   445	`NEXT_STEPS.md` lists this and four further items with refutation criteria and costs.
   446	
