# Track A: binary coefficient sections. Analysis (agent infraA)

> **r3 audit applied 2026-10-17 (agent `repair`).** Items corrected in place are marked "Corrected 2026-10-17 after r3 audit". The full adjudication is in `notes/repair_adjudication.md`. Reproductions: `src/repair/audit_trackA.py`, `tests/test_repair_audit.py`.

Status labels follow AGENT_RULES §5. Notation: `F_N = (1+X)^N - 1 - X^N = Σ_{0<i<N} a_i X^i`
over `R_N = Z/NZ`. Section `S_{r,M}(Y) = Σ_j a_{r+jM} Y^j`, with `M = 2^k` at level `k`.
Balanced family: `N = pq`, `p < q < 2p` (same bit length). Lucas support facts are in
`notes/seed_lemmas.md` (L7): `supp F_N = S_p ⊔ S_q`, `S_q = {qj : 1 ≤ j ≤ p-1}`,
`S_p = p·([1, q-p] ∪ [p, q-1])`, `|supp| = 2q - p - 1`.

Prior-art notes from the orchestrator (codex round 1, `notes/codex_r1_mechanisms.md`):
(i) randomized formal PIT over `R_N` via random monic quotients is known (Agrawal–Biswas;
Arvind–Mukhopadhyay–Srinivasan, arXiv 0801.0514 §6). (ii) The generating function
`Σ_t [X^r]((1+X)^t mod X^M - c) z^t = z^r(1-z)^{M-1-r}/((1-z)^M - c z^M)` gives a
dimension ≥ M bound for constant-coefficient linear realizations *in the exponent `t`*.
I checked (ii) numerically (A2 below) and do not claim it. Theorem A1 is a different
statement: it works in the coefficient-index direction for the single fixed exponent `N`,
and it covers nonlinear evaluation (repeated squaring), arbitrary finite algebras, towers,
products, support-aware access and multilinear read-outs. §3.4 states exactly what
goes beyond (ii).

## 0. Summary of results

| # | Statement | Status |
|---|---|---|
| A1 | Dimension conservation: any observable `λ(f(x))`, `x` in an `R_N`-algebra generated by `D` elements, that isolates residue class `r mod M` and is nonzero on some `X^i`, `i ≡ r`, `i ≥ M`, needs `D ≥ M`. This covers towers (`M ≤ quotient dim × extension degree`), products (`Σ` ranks), `R_N[X,Y]` quotients, multilinear read-outs, and the support-aware version (vanishing required only on multiples of a hidden prime). **Corrected 2026-10-17 after r3 audit:** the hypothesis `i ≥ M` is essential. Without it the bound is `D ≥ min(M, i+1)` (§3.3). | **proved** (with the index hypothesis) |
| A2 | The exponent-direction linear recurrence of a section has order exactly `M` (codex r1 §4.2, not ours). | checked numerically 45/45 |
| E1 | Mask corollary: an isolating observable for a mask with a zero-run of length `g` before a nontrivial index needs rank `≥ g+1`. Bit-`j` masks need `≥ 2^j+1`, class masks `≥ M`, interval `[a,b)` needs `≥ a+1`. Over a field (squarefree `N`), prefix/interval masks need `≥` (last nontrivial index + 1) **provided the isolation region contains a trailing zero block of length ≥ rank after that index** (corrected 2026-10-17 after r3 audit; see §7 for the F_5 counterexample without it). The upper bounds are attained. | **proved**; exhaustive F_2/F_3 checks, 38/38 tight |
| C1 | Balanced `N`, any `M ≤ p-1` coprime to `q`: every residue class is hit, so both children are always nonzero for the first `⌊log2(p-1)⌋` levels. | **proved**, checked to 40 bits |
| C2 | Balanced `N`, `M = 2^k > p` (more generally `M > p` with `gcd(M, N) = 1`): every section has at most 3 nonzero terms (≤ 1 from `S_q`, ≤ 2 from `S_p`). **Scope corrected 2026-10-17 after r3 audit:** false for `M` sharing a factor with `N`; e.g. `N = 35, M = 7, r = 0` has the 4 terms 7, 14, 21, 28. | **proved** (binary / coprime `M`), checked to 40 bits and all N ≤ 5000; coprime `M`: 217 772 cases, `N ≤ 3000`, 0 violations |
| C3 | A *pure* class (hit by one prime's support only) has values `≡ 0` mod the other prime, so `gcd(S_{r,M}(c), N)` is a proper factor **whenever `S_{r,M}(c) ≢ 0` mod the supporting prime** (gap fixed 2026-10-17 after r3 audit: `N = 35, M = 8, r = 1` gives `21Y^3`, whose value at `c = 0` has gcd 35. A good `c` exists by C4). Pure classes require `M > min(p-1, q-p)`, and the first one appears at `M ∈ (q-p, 4(q-p)]` in all 25 descent runs. The cost is Fermat-like, no gain. | necessity **proved**; threshold **experimentally supported** |
| C4 | **Odd** balanced semiprime, `M = 2^k ≥ 2`: every nonzero section is nonzero at some scalar `c ∈ R_N`. There is no "561 trap" at any level of this family. **Corrected 2026-10-17 after r3 audit:** (a) the printed proof inequality was false; the repaired span argument is in §4. (b) `N = 6` (even) is a counterexample: `S_{0,2} = 3Y + 3Y^2` vanishes on `Z/6`. (c) The earlier sentence "the trap exists only at `M = 1` and only for Carmichael `N`" is **wrong** outside this family. `S_{0,4}(F_51) = 34Y^3+34Y^6+17Y^9+17Y^12` is a scalar trap, and there are 744 traps with `M = 2^k ≥ 2` among odd squarefree `N ≤ 1500` (none balanced). | **proved** (odd balanced, binary `M`); census 0 false zeros in 44 110 nonzero sections; repair re-check: 0 in 5 246 (odd balanced `N ≤ 1200`, exhaustive `c`) |
| D | Best selector found (exact scalar evaluation, rank `2M`) is correct at every level (25/25 runs), but its cost per level doubles exactly, so it is **exponential**: total `Θ(N log N)` ring-op·dimension to full depth, worse than trial division. **Corrected 2026-10-17 after r3 audit:** the budget selector *is* a coin by construction once `2M > D` (`BudgetSelector` returns `rng.getrandbits(1)`), so its coin-flip rate is not evidence. A1 does not predict coin-flip behaviour for general rank-`D` rules (failure to isolate ≠ indistinguishability). The cost doubling is a property of this selector. A1 gives only rank `≥ min(2M, p+1)` (§3.3). | **experimentally supported** |
| Circ | Hash-consed E/O transforms on the repeated-squaring circuit grow ×3.6–3.7 per level (naive, → 4) and ×3.0 (Karatsuba gate, → 3), identically on diagnostic and operational paths. At level 9 (M=512 > p) the circuit of a ≤3-term polynomial has 1.5·10^6 nodes. | **experimentally supported** (measurement of this implementation; the "mechanism" in §2 is a heuristic, not a proof. Corrected 2026-10-17 after r3 audit) |

**Bottom line.** Within the access model that every cheap quotient evaluation lives in,
*certifying* a nonzero child at modulus `M` through an isolating observable costs algebra rank
`≥ min(M, i+1)`, where `i` is a support index of the child. **Corrected 2026-10-17 after r3 audit:** the
earlier "`≥ 2^k`" dropped A1's index hypothesis. A counterexample is `N = 35, M = 16, r = 5` via `R_35[X]/(X^6)`.
In the balanced family every support index is `≥ p`. So the first `≈ log2 p` levels need no
selection (C1), and every later level (`M ≥ p+1`) needs rank `≥ p+1` (§3.3, Corollary 3′). The
bound is rank `> p`, flat in `k`; it does not double. It constrains only selectors that read isolating observables,
and rank is not a bit-cost lower bound without a dense-representation assumption (§3.1). Zero testing is *not* the obstacle in this family: by C2 and C4 the sections that need testing are
≤3-sparse and never scalar-false-zero, and general PIT over `R_N` is known anyway. What remains open
is a **non-uniform, binomial-specific** construction of section circuits (escape route E-1 below).
Our measurements show that the generic gate-local transform does not provide it.

---

## 1. Setup and the roots-of-unity closed form (task b)

* `S_{r,M}(Y)` is the `X^r`-coordinate of `(1+X)^N` in `R_N[Y][X]/(X^M - Y) ≅ R_N[X]` (minus
  endpoint terms). Scalar evaluation `S_{r,M}(c)` is coefficient `r` of `(1+X)^N mod (N, X^M - c)`.
  It is computed by repeated squaring in a rank-`M` algebra: `≈1.5 log2 N` products of
  degree-`<M` polynomials (`src/trackA/sections.py: section_values`). **proved** (definition),
  tested against explicit binomials.
* Roots-of-unity filter: over any ring containing `1/M` and a primitive `M`-th root `ω`,
  `S_{r,M}(t^M) = M^{-1} t^{-r} Σ_{i<M} ω^{-ir} (1+ω^i t)^N`. Over `R_N` the `ω` live in
  `R_N[Z]/(Φ_M)` (rank `M/2`) and `t` lives in `R_N[T]/(T^M - c)` (rank `M`), so the filter is never
  cheaper than the direct quotient. Taking `c = t_0^M` with `t_0 ∈ R_N` gives the identity
  `t_0^r S_{r,M}(t_0^M) = [Z^r]((1+t_0 Z)^N mod Z^M - 1)`, which is again rank `M`.
* Descent: `X^{2M} - s^2 = (X^M - s)(X^M + s)`, and by CRT (when `2s` is a unit)
  `R_N[X]/(X^{2M}-s^2) ≅ R_N[X]/(X^M-s) × R_N[X]/(X^M+s)`. The children of class `r` at `c = s^2` are
  `S_{r,2M}(s^2) = (P(s)+P(-s))/2` and `S_{r+M,2M}(s^2) = (P(s)-P(-s))/(2s)` with `P = S_{r,M}`
  (`descent_split`, gcd-checked: a nonunit `2s` is returned as `NonUnit`). The tower form
  `R_N[s]/(s^2-c)[X]/(X^M - s)` is isomorphic to `R_N[X]/(X^{2M}-c)` via `s ↦ X^M`. In every form the
  rank is `2M`, so "conservation" holds with equality in the canonical construction. **proved**, tested.
* Equivalently: child 0 vanishes iff `P` is odd (`P(-Y) = -P(Y)`), and child 1 vanishes iff `P` is even.
  So the child test is a parity test on the parent. It requires `P(-c)`, which lives in a *different*
  rank-`M` algebra `R_N[X]/(X^M + c)`. Relating the two algebras needs a primitive `2M`-th root of unity
  (rank `M` extension).

## 2. Circuit section transforms (task a)

Implementation: `src/circuit_sections.py`. Circuits are hash-consed DAGs over `R_N`,
with constant folding (`0+a`, `0·a`, `1·a`, const∘const). One level maps each gate `v` to
`(e(v), o(v))` by the BRIEF gate rules. A mul gate costs 4 products (naive) or 3 (Karatsuba:
`O(ab) = (e_a+o_a)(e_b+o_b) - e_a e_b - o_a o_b`, sharing `e_a e_b`, `o_a o_b` with `E`).
Only the chosen section is kept, and it is pruned to reachable nodes before the next level.
Correctness is checked against explicit coefficients (tests).

